## Additional context: multiple published plugins already rely on this pattern

This isn't just a hypothetical — the `config(cfg)` mutation pattern is already widely used across the plugin ecosystem. Here are five concrete examples from published plugins:

---

### 1. [slkiser/opencode-quota](https://github.com/slkiser/opencode-quota) — `src/plugin.ts`

The most popular quota/usage tracking plugin registers **11+ slash commands** via mutation:

```ts
config: async (input: unknown) => {
  const cfg = input as PluginConfigInput;
  cfg.command ??= {};
  cfg.command["quota"] = { template: "/quota", description: "..." };
  cfg.command["quota_status"] = { ... };
  cfg.command["pricing_refresh"] = { ... };
  // registers all /tokens_* commands in a loop
  for (const spec of TOKEN_REPORT_COMMANDS) {
    cfg.command[spec.id] = { template: spec.template, description: spec.description };
  }
}
```

---

### 2. [timrichardson/opencode-planner](https://github.com/timrichardson/opencode-planner) — `index.js`

Registers commands, a custom agent, and mutates existing agents' permissions:

```js
async config(cfg) {
  cfg.agent ??= {};
  cfg.command = {
    "planner-config": plannerConfigCommand,
    "edit-plan": editPlanCommand,
    ...cfg.command,
  };
  cfg.agent[agent] = mode(cfg.agent[agent]);        // registers "plan" agent
  cfg.agent.general = restrictPlannerSubagent(...);  // modifies existing agents
  cfg.agent.explore = restrictPlannerSubagent(...);
}
```

---

### 3. [khoafullstack/opencode-kit](https://github.com/khoafullstack/opencode-kit) — `src/index.ts`

A bundled toolkit that mutates **all four config sections** (skills, mcp, agents, commands):

```ts
async config(input) {
  const cfg = input as { skills?, mcp?, agent?, command? };
  cfg.skills.paths.push(skillsRoot);
  for (const [name, server] of Object.entries(bundledMcp))
    cfg.mcp[name] = server;
  for (const [name, agent] of Object.entries(bundledAgents))
    cfg.agent[name] = agent;
  for (const [name, command] of Object.entries(bundledCommands))
    cfg.command[name] = command;
}
```

---

### 4. [Helweg/opencode-codebase-index](https://github.com/Helweg/opencode-codebase-index)

Registers commands loaded dynamically from a directory:

```js
async config(cfg) {
  cfg.command = cfg.command ?? {};
  const commands = loadCommandsFromDirectory(commandsDir);
  for (const [name, definition] of commands) {
    cfg.command[name] = definition;
  }
}
```

---

### 5. [anand-testcompare/opencode-palantir](https://github.com/anand-testcompare/opencode-palantir)

Registers both commands and agents through helper functions:

```js
config: async (cfg) => {
  ensureCommandDefinitions(cfg);  // registers 4 slash commands
  ensureAgentDefinitions(cfg);    // registers "foundry-librarian" + "foundry" agents
}
```

---

## Summary

All five plugins assume:
- `cfg` is a mutable object
- mutations to `cfg.agent`, `cfg.command`, `cfg.mcp`, and `cfg.skills.paths` persist and take effect
- the config hook is the canonical way to register plugin-owned artifacts

None of this is documented. If the pattern is going to be deprecated or changed, that would be a breaking change for a significant portion of the plugin ecosystem.

If it's going to stay (intentionally or by policy), it deserves first-class documentation so it can be relied on with confidence.

@frost19k this seems to be an issue with how OpenCode parses paths inside permissions. For some reason, for `Read` they are parsed as absolute paths, but for `Edit` they are parsed as paths relative to the current working folder.

In `packages/opencode/src/tool/read.ts`: filename is passed as absolute to permission check in [line 182](https://github.com/anomalyco/opencode/blob/86715fecc469e7bf12e526d386e4927afc95fa3e/packages/opencode/src/tool/read.ts#L180)

In `packages/opencode/src/tool/edit.ts`: filename is passed as relative to permission check in [line 55](https://github.com/anomalyco/opencode/blob/86715fecc469e7bf12e526d386e4927afc95fa3e/packages/opencode/src/tool/write.ts#L55)

